  </section>

  <section class="wrap band">
    <h2>Why Tur</h2>
    <p class="band__lead">Pick Tur if you want one of these on an Apple Silicon Mac.</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Many clients, one model</h3>
        <p>Several clients or agents share each model pass, so eight sequences decode {{ perf.stats.batched }}% faster than on llama.cpp on the same Mac.</p>
        <a href="/docs/architecture/scheduling/">Scheduling</a>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Models larger than memory</h3>
        <p>Tur keeps most of a mixture-of-experts model on the SSD and reads experts into a fixed cache, with several requests at once and no SSD writes during a run.</p>
        <a href="/docs/architecture/expert-cache/">Expert cache</a>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>A server you can leave running</h3>
        <p>Tur plans all its memory before loading a weight, so a configuration that doesn't fit fails at startup, and one failed request doesn't affect the others.</p>
        <a href="/docs/architecture/memory/">State memory</a>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band">
    <h2>How one pass works</h2>
    <p class="band__lead">Producing a token on a Mac is mostly a memory problem: the GPU spends more time waiting for weights and request state than computing with them. Tur shapes each layer to read fewer bytes.</p>
    <ol class="pass">
      <li class="pass__step">
        <div class="pass__art pack" aria-hidden="true">
          <i class="a"></i><i class="b"></i><i class="c"></i><i class="d"></i><i class="p"></i><i class="p"></i><i class="p"></i><i class="p"></i>
        </div>
        <h3>Pack</h3>
        <p>Every running request's next token joins chunks of new prompts in one execution with no padding.</p>
        <a href="/docs/architecture/execution/">Execution engine</a>
      </li>
      <li class="pass__step">
        <div class="pass__art weights" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
        <h3>Read weights once</h3>
        <p>Fused Metal kernels read the weights once for the whole batch. A custom kernel stays only where it beats Candle's.</p>
        <a href="/docs/architecture/kernels/">Metal kernels</a>
      </li>
      <li class="pass__step">
        <div class="pass__art pool" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i class="on"></i><i></i><i class="on"></i><i class="on"></i><i></i><i class="on"></i><i></i><i></i><i class="on"></i><i class="on"></i><i></i></div>
        <h3>Read state in place</h3>
        <p>Attention and recurrent state live in fixed pools, so nothing is copied or reassembled per token.</p>
        <a href="/docs/architecture/memory/">State memory</a>
      </li>
      <li class="pass__step">
        <div class="pass__art sample" aria-hidden="true"><i style="--h:30%"></i><i style="--h:85%" class="on"></i><i style="--h:50%"></i><i style="--h:20%"></i><i style="--h:40%"></i></div>
        <h3>Sample on the GPU</h3>
        <p>Sampling runs right after the model pass, and only the chosen token returns to the CPU.</p>
        <a href="/docs/architecture/sampling/">Sampling</a>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="band__after">Two more mechanisms skip work without changing the generated text. <a href="/docs/architecture/prefix-reuse/">Prefix reuse</a> computes a shared system prompt once, and <a href="/docs/architecture/speculation/">speculative decoding</a> drafts several tokens and verifies them in one pass.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap band">
    <h2>Watch the tokens</h2>
    <p class="band__lead">Add sequences and see total throughput climb. Each row is one request, replayed at the rate measured on that Mac. No model runs in your browser.</p>
    <div class="lab" data-lab="race"></div>
  </section>

